  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,5]?$",
  "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,5]$ and differentiable on $(0,5).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[0,5]$ and differentiable on $(0,5)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=1.$ Hence, $q(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,5).$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$  is continuous on $[0,5]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$  Hence, $r(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,5).$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, $s(x)$ is not continuous on $[0,5].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[0,5]$ but has corner points at $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2$ and $x=4,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,5).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.",
